            "text": "ROOT CAUSE:\nThe `if` condition checks `b < a` instead of `b > a`, which causes `max` to be updated with the smaller number instead of the larger one.\n\nBEGINNER EXPLANATION:\nWhen finding the maximum of two numbers, you want to replace your current maximum (`a`) with `b` only if `b` is strictly greater than `a`. In your code, the condition `b < a` checks if `b` is smaller than `a`, so the program ends up printing the minimum value instead of the maximum. Changing `<` to `>` fixes the problem.\n\nPATCHED CODE:\n```java\nimport java.util.Scanner;\npublic class J07 {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n        int a = sc.nextInt();\n        int b = sc.nextInt();\n        int max = a;\n        if (b > a) {\n            max = b;\n        }\n        System.out.println(max);\n    }\n}\n```",
